    "local": {
      "heading": "What makes an Ohio replacement different",
      "paras": [
        "Ohio leans gas: 57% of homes heat water with natural gas and 38% with electricity, and gas is priced at $1.30 per therm. Utility help is thinner than in most states we cover: Columbia Gas of Ohio has ended its residential efficiency rebates except for income-qualified weatherization, and the state's federal rebate status could not be confirmed.",
        "Incoming water drops to about 46.9 °F in February, so plan a tankless unit for winter flow, and check that a basement heat pump location stays above 40 °F. A plain gas tank, about $1,743 installed, stays the cheapest to own over 10 years."
      ]
    },
    "highlights": [
      "Ohio is one of the cheaper states for installation labor: its price level is the 17th lowest of the 50 states and DC, so a gas tank's labor runs about $603 against $650 nationally.",
      "Where you live inside Ohio matters: the same 50-gallon gas job is about $1,768 in the Columbus, OH area and $1,694 in the Youngstown-Warren, OH area.",
      "Cleveland requires a permit for a replaced water heater."
    ]
